sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Me
    participant C as Debug container (NAS)
    participant F as Image Factory
    participant D as Node (Debian)
    participant T as Node (Talos)

    Note over Me,T: 1. Fetch the Talos kernel and initramfs
    D->>F: download kernel and initramfs for this node's schematic
    F-->>D: kernel-amd64, initramfs-amd64.xz

    Note over Me,T: 2. kexec into maintenance mode
    Me->>D: kexec -l, sync, kexec -e
    D->>T: jump into the Talos kernel, nothing written to disk

    Note over Me,T: 3. Inspect, configure, install (talosctl runs on the LAN)
    Me->>C: talosctl get disks, get links
    C->>T: query the node (insecure)
    T-->>C: disk serials and NIC names
    Me->>C: gen secrets, gen config, validate
    Me->>C: apply-config
    C->>T: apply-config (insecure)
    T->>T: install on the unused disk, reboot into it
    Me->>C: bootstrap, kubeconfig
    C->>T: bootstrap

    Note over Me,T: 4. Back to Debian
    Me->>C: talosctl reboot --mode powercycle
    C->>T: power cycle, skipping kexec
    T->>D: firmware boots Debian, boot order unchanged

    Note over Me,T: 5. Boot Talos for one boot only
    Me->>D: efibootmgr -v, find the Talos entry
    Me->>D: efibootmgr --bootnext, reboot
    D->>T: next boot goes to Talos
    Me->>C: check that Talos is healthy
    Me->>C: talosctl reboot --mode powercycle
    C->>T: power cycle
    T->>D: BootNext is one-shot, so Debian is back

    Note over Me,T: 6. Make Talos the default
    Me->>D: efibootmgr --bootorder, Talos first, reboot
    D->>T: firmware boots Talos by default

    Note over Me,T: 7. Once Talos is stable, wipe the Debian disk
